Reconnaissance completed, but the filesystem was unavailable: neither specified file could be inspected or updated. The recovery-ready units below should therefore be appended to the cumulative manifest and materials file; they must not replace existing contents.

Identity and authority: Félix-Antoine Tshisekedi Tshilombo was the serving president throughout 2022, within the five-year term beginning after the December 2018 election. By 2022, his practical central-government authority was materially stronger than at inauguration because the Sacred Union coalition had displaced Joseph Kabila’s parliamentary dominance. That authority remained constrained by a heterogeneous coalition, weak institutions, imperfect civilian control of security forces, armed groups controlling or contesting eastern territory, foreign intervention, and dependence on MONUSCO and regional forces.

The evidence environment is uneven. Kinshasa-level political, fiscal, and legal developments are relatively well documented. Eastern-conflict evidence is abundant but attribution is difficult: state forces, foreign forces, M23, ADF, local militias, and unidentified actors overlap. Government and advocacy reporting frequently disagree over responsibility. Rwanda denied supporting M23, contrary to UN-expert and independent reporting. National outcomes should not automatically be attributed personally to Tshisekedi, and none of the material located establishes a Chapter 7B personal-integrity finding.

Sources opened and retained include the DRC Presidency, Human Rights Watch’s 2022 retrospective, its state-of-siege investigation, the IMF’s July 2022 Article IV/ECF report, the World Bank’s 2022 governance-reform operation, UN Security Council material, RSF, CPJ, and Congo Research Group/Ebuteli.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-001","canonical_fact_key":"cod_tshisekedi_presidential_term_2022","disposition":"accept_context","chapter_ids":["1B","2B","3B","4B","5B","6B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.1","2B.1","3B.1","4B.1","5B.1","6B.1","7B.1","8B.1"],"url":"https://www.presidence.cd/president/le-president","claim":"The official presidency biography identifies Félix-Antoine Tshisekedi Tshilombo as elected president on 30 December 2018 for a five-year term, confirming that he held the presidency throughout 2022.","locator":"Lines 76-77, presidential biography; opened 2026-07-24","publisher":"Présidence de la République démocratique du Congo","publication_date":"undated","period_fit":"Direct identity and term fit for 2022.","attribution_limits":"Official source verifies officeholding but is not independent evidence of election quality, practical control, or performance.","contrary_evidence":"Independent observers disputed the announced 2018 result, but not that Tshisekedi exercised the presidency in 2022."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-002","canonical_fact_key":"cod_tshisekedi_sacred_union_practical_authority","disposition":"accept_context","chapter_ids":["1B","4B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["1B.1","1B.2","4B.2","7B.1","8B.1"],"url":"https://www.ecoi.net/en/document/2050895.html","claim":"International Crisis Group assessed that creation and investiture of the Sacred Union parliamentary majority ended the period in which Tshisekedi remained strongly constrained by former president Joseph Kabila, materially expanding Tshisekedi’s practical governing authority before 2022.","locator":"Summary paragraphs beginning 'After months of political manoeuvring' and 'The 26 April investiture'","publisher":"International Crisis Group, mirrored by ecoi.net","publication_date":"2021","period_fit":"Immediate institutional background governing the distribution of authority in 2022.","attribution_limits":"Establishes increased political capacity, not personal responsibility for every act of government or coalition member.","contrary_evidence":"The Sacred Union was heterogeneous and based partly on political defections, so a parliamentary majority did not eliminate coalition bargaining or weak state reach."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-003","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_state_of_siege_repression","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["3B","4B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.2","3B.4","4B.3","4B.4","8B.2","8B.4"],"url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/22/dr-congo-martial-law-brings-crackdown-east","claim":"Under the state of siege declared by Tshisekedi’s administration, military and police authorities in North Kivu and Ituri curtailed expression and assembly, arbitrarily detained or prosecuted critics, and used lethal force against at least one protester; military rule also transferred civilian authority to military officials.","locator":"Lines 616-635, especially 616-621 and 633-635","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-22","period_fit":"Direct reporting on conditions and incidents continuing into early 2022.","attribution_limits":"Supports an administration-level policy nexus and conduct by subordinate security authorities; it does not prove that Tshisekedi ordered individual abuses.","contrary_evidence":"The state of siege was officially justified as a response to severe armed-group insecurity; the source argues that restrictions were overbroad and that insecurity persisted."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-004","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_eastern_conflict_m23_civilian_harm","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["2B","3B","6B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.1","2B.2","2B.4","3B.1","3B.3","6B.2","8B.3"],"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"During 2022, roughly 120 armed groups operated across eastern provinces; the M23 mounted a major offensive and seized territory, while diverse armed actors killed at least 2,446 civilians in South Kivu, North Kivu, and Ituri between January and late October, including at least 155 attributed to Congolese security forces.","locator":"Events of 2022, lines 76-82, especially 78-82","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","period_fit":"Retrospective specifically labeled 'Events of 2022'.","attribution_limits":"Conflict totals combine many perpetrators and cannot be assigned personally to Tshisekedi; the security-force subset is narrower but still does not establish presidential orders.","contrary_evidence":"Rwanda denied supporting M23; attribution of individual killings was sometimes uncertain, and government forces faced major externally supported and non-state threats."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-005","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_displacement_and_security_constraint","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["2B","3B","6B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.3","3B.3","6B.1","6B.2","8B.3"],"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"Nearly 5.6 million people were displaced across the DRC by July 2022, including more than 1.6 million in Ituri and 1.8 million in North Kivu, while military rule failed to curb widespread violence.","locator":"Events of 2022, lines 60-68","publisher":"Human Rights Watch, citing United Nations displacement data","publication_date":"2023","period_fit":"Direct mid-2022 humanitarian condition.","attribution_limits":"The displacement burden reflects long-running conflict and numerous state, foreign, and non-state actors; it is a governing constraint and outcome, not automatically personal conduct.","contrary_evidence":"The administration pursued military, regional, and diplomatic responses, but the source reports that security continued deteriorating."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-006","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_macro_stabilization_and_reform","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["5B","6B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.1","5B.2","6B.3","7B.3","8B.5"],"url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2022/07/05/democratic-republic-of-the-congo-staff-report-for-the-2022-article-iv-consultation-second-520400","claim":"The IMF found that the DRC’s macroeconomic environment had improved since 2019 and credited prudent policies, most visibly the cessation of central-bank financing of government, while emphasizing that governance, natural-resource management, business-climate, and structural reforms remained necessary for inclusive growth.","locator":"Summary, lines 268-271","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-07-05","period_fit":"Contemporaneous assessment covering policy performance through the first half of 2022.","attribution_limits":"Assessment concerns the authorities collectively; it should not be converted into sole personal credit for Tshisekedi or into household-welfare conclusions.","contrary_evidence":"The IMF explicitly identified unfinished structural and governance reforms and exposure to external shocks."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-007","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_foundational_governance_reforms","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["5B","6B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.2","5B.4","6B.3","7B.2","7B.3","8B.5"],"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/674011655914223908/pdf/Congo-Democratic-Republic-of-Foundational-Economic-Governance-Reforms-Development-Policy-Financing.pdf","claim":"A 2022 World Bank operation recorded a government reform program covering public expenditure, mining royalties, telecom liberalization, state-owned-enterprise transparency, and forest governance; it described satisfactory implementation of IMF-program measures and completion of prior actions, while also documenting major baseline transparency and institutional deficits.","locator":"PDF pages 4-10, especially pages 7-8 lines 200-301 and page 13 lines 517-538","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022","period_fit":"Contemporaneous program document and prior-action assessment.","attribution_limits":"Program completion is government-level evidence and may reflect conditionality and work by ministries; it does not prove broad implementation, downstream effectiveness, or presidential integrity.","contrary_evidence":"The document’s baselines show missing public disclosure, weak royalty tracking, questionable forest concessions, poor SOE performance, and long-pending reforms."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-008","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_debt_disclosure_and_fiscal_constraint","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["5B","6B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.2","5B.3","6B.3","7B.3","8B.5"],"url":"https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/674011655914223908/pdf/Congo-Democratic-Republic-of-Foundational-Economic-Governance-Reforms-Development-Policy-Financing.pdf","claim":"By March 2022 the government had published an annual public-debt report and a quarterly bulletin including strategic state-owned-enterprise debt, satisfying a World Bank transparency action; debt distress risk remained moderate and the economy remained vulnerable to commodity prices, conflict, food and fuel inflation, and weak household buffers.","locator":"PDF pages 21-22, lines 765-819, especially 783-800 and 811-819","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022","period_fit":"Specific actions and constraints documented for 2022.","attribution_limits":"A bounded transparency improvement, not evidence that all public liabilities or spending were transparent; collective government action rather than personal conduct.","contrary_evidence":"Stress tests breached some thresholds, and the report described high social risk among a largely poor and food-insecure population."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-009","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_indigenous_peoples_law","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["3B","4B","6B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.5","4B.5","6B.4","8B.6"],"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"In November 2022, Tshisekedi signed a law on the protection and promotion of the rights of Indigenous Pygmy peoples.","locator":"Events of 2022, Environment and Human Rights, lines 104-110, especially line 108","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","period_fit":"Specific presidential legal act in November 2022.","attribution_limits":"Signing establishes a direct presidential nexus but not implementation, enforcement, resource allocation, or realized outcomes.","contrary_evidence":"The same source reports environmental and concession policies that could threaten forest-dependent communities, so the law should not be treated as proof of overall rights protection."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-010","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_oil_gas_block_auction","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["5B","6B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["5B.4","6B.4","8B.6"],"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"In July 2022 the government launched an auction for 27 oil and three gas blocks, potentially opening about 11 million hectares of rainforest to drilling, while an earlier official audit had identified illegal logging permits and widespread tax avoidance by concession holders.","locator":"Events of 2022, lines 104-110, especially 107 and 110","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","period_fit":"Direct 2022 policy action and contemporaneously released audit context.","attribution_limits":"This is an administration-level resource-development policy; environmental effects were prospective and the source’s emissions estimate is a modeled risk, not a realized 2022 outcome.","contrary_evidence":"The government suspended 12 forest concessions and framed resource development as an economic opportunity, but did not cancel all permits identified as illegal."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-011","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_accountability_mixed_record","disposition":"accept_with_integrity_caveat","chapter_ids":["3B","4B","7B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["3B.4","4B.4","7B.1","7B.4","8B.2"],"url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/democratic-republic-congo","claim":"The administration made little progress on systemic reforms addressing corruption and impunity; in 2022 an appellate court acquitted former presidential chief of staff Vital Kamerhe after his earlier conviction for embezzlement, while major accountability cases remained incomplete.","locator":"Events of 2022, lines 60-63 and Justice and Accountability section beginning line 88; page text concerning Kamerhe","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023","period_fit":"Retrospective account of 2022.","attribution_limits":"The acquittal and weak accountability record do not establish corruption by Tshisekedi, interference by him, or a Chapter 7B personal-integrity nexus. Treat solely as institutional context pending court records and evidence of presidential involvement.","contrary_evidence":"Kamerhe’s acquittal is itself contrary to treating the earlier conviction as a settled finding; some anti-corruption and governance reforms proceeded under IMF and World Bank programs."}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"provisional_id":"RECON-AC-2022-A-012","canonical_fact_key":"cod_2022_information_environment_mixed_threat_actors","disposition":"accept","chapter_ids":["2B","3B","4B","8B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.3","3B.2","4B.3","8B.2"],"url":"https://cpj.org/2022/11/congolese-journalists-face-death-threats-as-m23-rebels-advance-flee-for-safety/","claim":"At least 23 journalists from Kiwanja and Rutshuru sought MONUSCO protection after M23 captured the towns in late October 2022, and journalists reported receiving death threats attributed to M23 members.","locator":"Opening paragraphs under the 3 November 2022 dateline","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-11-03","period_fit":"Direct reporting on the late-2022 M23 offensive.","attribution_limits":"Shows that the information environment was also degraded by non-state armed actors; it cannot be attributed to Tshisekedi or state policy.","contrary_evidence":"Separate HRW and CPJ reporting documents state and security-force restrictions and attacks, so non-state threats do not negate government responsibility for its own conduct."}

Chapter gaps for the compact follow-up sessions:

- 1B: verify 2022 executive decision-making, cabinet and coalition constraints, appointments, crisis coordination, and credible evidence of strategic prioritization.
- 2B: separate conflict initiation, prevention, diplomacy, civilian protection, FARDC conduct, regional-force decisions, and inherited structural conflict.
- 3B: obtain UNJHRO/OHCHR 2022 incident data and judicial outcomes; distinguish armed-group crimes from state violations and presidentially linked policy.
- 4B: examine electoral-law and CENI disputes, opposition treatment, judicial independence, parliamentary checks, and any positive opening of civic space.
- 5B: verify full-year growth, inflation, fiscal execution, mining dependence, employment and distributional outcomes; distinguish mining-led growth from broad welfare.
- 6B: investigate free-primary-education implementation, health financing, food insecurity, displacement services, gender outcomes, and Indigenous-law implementation.
- 7B: no personal-integrity nexus has yet been established. Search asset declarations, procurement records, IGF findings, court records, conflicts of interest, and documented presidential responses—without treating associates’ allegations as his conduct.
- 8B: test implementation and durability of reforms, completion against stated targets, administrative capacity outside Kinshasa, security-policy adaptation, and credible instances where the government corrected failure.
